Yes. For a lightweight, dependency-free setup, use an in-memory `Map` with a time-to-live (TTL). This avoids repeated API calls during a session without adding app size. A five-minute TTL is a reasonable starting point; you can increase it to save more of your 1,000 daily calls, or shorten it when fresher weather matters more.

```javascript
const weatherCache = new Map();
const CACHE_TTL_MS = 5 * 60 * 1000; // 5 minutes

async function getWeather(city) {
  const key = city.trim().toLowerCase();
  const cached = weatherCache.get(key);

  if (cached && cached.expiresAt > Date.now()) {
    return cached.data;
  }

  // Remove an expired entry, if present.
  weatherCache.delete(key);

  const apiKey = "my_api_key";
  const url =
    `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${encodeURIComponent(city)}` +
    `&appid=${apiKey}`;

  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Weather request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const data = await response.json();
  weatherCache.set(key, {
    data,
    expiresAt: Date.now() + CACHE_TTL_MS
  });

  return data;
}
```

This cache lasts only until the page is closed or reloaded. If you want cached results to survive reloads, use `localStorage` instead, storing the response alongside an expiration timestamp. For your app, keep the cache bounded—such as retaining only the three most recently searched cities—so it stays small.
